  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "input colors ordered from most frequent to least frequent by pixel count",
  "dst": "contiguous runs in a same-size grid scanned column-major left-to-right and bottom-to-top within each column",
  "match_cond": "the k-th ranked color fills the k-th scan-order run, with run length equal to its input pixel count",
  "transform": "discard original positions and preserve the exact pixel count of each color"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10x10 [observed]
  colors:
    count: exactly 4 distinct non-zero colors (from 1-9)
    pixel_counts: all distinct; sum to grid_area (100)
    ranked_distribution:
      1st: 44-51 pixels
      2nd: 29 pixels [observed]
      3rd: 14-19 pixels
      4th: 2-8 pixels
  input:
    spatial: colors scattered across grid in multiple disconnected components (3-11 each)
    no_structure: positions are essentially random; only counts matter
  output:
    packing: colors placed as contiguous runs scanning columns left-to-right, bottom-to-top within each column
    order: most frequent color first, least frequent last
    result: output preserves exact pixel counts but reorganizes into sorted column packing

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct random generation (task is simple enough)
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size 10x10
    2. Pick 4 distinct colors from 1-9
    3. Choose 4 distinct pixel counts that sum to 100, with largest in 40-55, second in 25-35, third in 10-20, fourth in 2-10
    4. Randomly assign each cell a color by shuffling a flat list of (count_i copies of color_i)
    5. Verify all counts are distinct (guaranteed by construction)
  validation:
    - All 4 counts are distinct
    - Counts sum to grid_area
    - No color 0 present
```
